# dyn.md D-1: a static-pie's `.dynsym` holds the null entry only, so
# `--export-dynamic` (which asks for every defined global in it) is
# accepted and has no effect, as `--export-dynamic-symbol` is, by name or
# by glob. Measured: GNU ld, lld and mold export several hundred symbols
# (GNU ld's x86-64 output then crashes at start-up; lld's and mold's
# run). `gcc -static-pie -rdynamic` passes no `--export-dynamic`.
#
# WP-056 measured the exports useless (glibc 2.41, AArch64 and x86-64): a
# library the static-pie `dlopen`s does not bind to the program's
# symbols (`undefined symbol: exe_value`, with GNU ld's, lld's and mold's
# exporting outputs alike) and `dlsym(RTLD_DEFAULT, ...)` does not find
# them. mortar refused the option by name until the verifier judged the
# line (P-D14 and A34, WP-088); the refusal went at that package's merge.
#
# Stage 3 judges it (spec/plumb.md §12.4, WP-089): GNU ld's static-pie
# carries A30 on AArch64 (a RELATIVE on the GOT slot of glibc's absolute
# `_nl_current_*_used`) and A31 on x86-64 (crtbeginS's `.plt.got` entry
# of `__cxa_finalize` through the slot plus 1); plumb.md §10.2.
#@ anomaly(bfd,aarch64): A30
#@ anomaly(bfd,x86_64): A31
#@ spec: dyn.md "D-1"
#@ arch: aarch64 x86_64

cc -O1 -fPIE main.c -o main.o
link(mortar) gcc -static-pie -Wl,--export-dynamic main.o -o spie
run(mortar) spie
  stdout: 0
check(mortar) readelf -W --dyn-syms spie
  has: Symbol table '.dynsym' contains 1 entry:
  not: main$
link gcc -static-pie -rdynamic main.o -o spie-r
run spie-r
  stdout: 0
check readelf -W --dyn-syms spie-r
  has(lld,mold,mortar): Symbol table '.dynsym' contains 1 entry:
  has(bfd,x86_64): Symbol table '.dynsym' contains 1 entry:
  has(bfd,aarch64): Symbol table '.dynsym' contains 3 entries:
link(mortar) gcc -static-pie -Wl,--export-dynamic-symbol=main -Wl,--export-dynamic-symbol=m* main.o -o spie-s
run(mortar) spie-s
  stdout: 0
check(mortar) readelf -W --dyn-syms spie-s
  has: Symbol table '.dynsym' contains 1 entry:
  not: main$

#--- main.c
#include <stdio.h>

int main(int argc, char **argv) {
  (void)argv;
  printf("%d\n", argc - 1);
  return 0;
}
